ory  Susie Snyder's here with Bank on the  bomb if you go to Bank on the bombs  webpage they have a  couple dozen companies involved in  producing nukes details of the contracts  it's a handy little guide of kind of the  Domesday corporations now I don't have a  lot of time left but I'll say two things  one is even at these huge levels of  spending the Pentagon can't afford  everything at once  I'll get the bomber as an example the  Air Force wants to buy the f-35 combat  aircrafts the most expensive weapons  program in history they want to buy a  new tanker they want to buy a new  training aircraft they want to buy new  drones and then they also want to fit in  one hundred billion dollars for a bomber  so there's going to be some fights even  within the military-industrial complex  but whose ox gets gored they're not  going to be rolling in the same  direction and those divisions could work  to our advantage now my final point is  you know a trillion dollars kind of  makes your eyes glaze over  because who who knows what that is but  the future of life Institute has this  fun little tool where you can go  shopping for different things until you  spend your trillion dollars so I went  shopping I hate shopping but I went  shopping and so a hundred million school  lunches is 235 million dollars ten  thousand high school science teachers  five hundred fifty three million dollars  salvage and protect all of our Superfund  sites for a year 681 million dollars  Planned Parenthood for a year five  hundred twenty-eight million dollars  health insurance for a million families  sixteen point eight billion dollars and  homelessness for a year twenty billion  dollars fix all our deficient bridges 71  billion dollars so as I said I hate  shopping so I got kind of exhausted but  that was only ten percent of the  trillion all of those things but there  was another good idea which was burn a 1  million dollar pile of cash every hour  for 30 years  262 billion dollars which is a far